{
  "id": "COMPOUND:537 - PROTEIN:473",
  "verbalization": "Acetohydroxamic acid appears to be a target of multiple pathways that ultimately lead to Diacylglycerol kinase gamma. The evidence suggests that acetohydroxamic acid interacts with G-protein coupled estrogen receptor 1 and 72 kDa type IV collagenase, both of which are targets of the same compound, N-[(3-hydroxy-4-methoxyphenyl)methyl]-7-methyloctanamide. This compound, in turn, is a common target of acetohydroxamic acid across different pathways, indicating a potential shared mechanism of action. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for N-[(3-hydroxy-4-methoxyphenyl)methyl]-7-methyloctanamide as a bridge between acetohydroxamic acid and Diacylglycerol kinase gamma, possibly through the activation of G-protein coupled receptors. The evidence supports a mechanism where acetohydroxamic acid may influence downstream signaling pathways that ultimately affect Diacylglycerol kinase gamma, though the exact nature of this interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
